Below is a **clean step-by-step SSH Remote Port Forwarding guide (Basic → Advanced)** for your exact setup:

* 🖥️ Client: `192.168.83.40`
* 🖥️ Server: `192.168.83.50`

---

# 🔐 REMOTE PORT FORWARDING (Client → Server)

👉 Means:
**Client (192.168.83.40) exposes a service to Server (192.168.83.50)**

---

# 🟢 BASIC SETUP

---

## 🧭 STEP 1 — Start service on CLIENT (192.168.83.40)

Example:

* Web app running on client

```text id="b1"
Client: 192.168.83.40:8090
```

✔ This is the service you want to expose

---

## 🧭 STEP 2 — Create reverse tunnel (run on CLIENT)

```bash id="b2"
ssh -R 9090:localhost:8090 user@192.168.83.50
```

---

## 🧠 Meaning:

| Part             | Meaning                |
| ---------------- | ---------------------- |
| `-R`             | Remote port forwarding |
| `9090`           | PORT opened on SERVER  |
| `localhost:8090` | Service on CLIENT      |
| `192.168.83.50`  | SSH SERVER             |

---

## 🔄 FLOW:

```text id="b3"
Client (192.168.83.40:8090)
        ↓ SSH Tunnel
Server (192.168.83.50:9090)
        ↓
Access from server browser
```

---

## 🧭 STEP 3 — Access from SERVER

On server:

```text id="b4"
http://localhost:9090
```

✔ You will see client service

---

# 🟡 INTERMEDIATE LEVEL

---

## 🔹 Allow external access on server (not just localhost)

```bash id="b5"
ssh -R 0.0.0.0:9090:localhost:8090 user@192.168.83.50
```

---

### What changes:

| Mode           | Access                             |
| -------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| localhost only | only server can access             |
| 0.0.0.0        | all machines in network can access |

---

## 🌐 Then access from other systems:

```text id="b6"
http://192.168.83.50:9090
```

---

# 🔵 ADVANCED LEVEL

---

## 1. Multiple services (same tunnel)

```bash id="b7"
ssh -R 9090:localhost:8090 \
    -R 15432:localhost:5432 \
    user@192.168.83.50
```

✔ Web + DB from client exposed to server

---

## 2. Background reverse tunnel

```bash id="b8"
ssh -f -N -R 9090:localhost:8090 user@192.168.83.50
```

### Meaning:

* `-f` → run in background
* `-N` → no shell, only tunnel

---

## 3. Persistent reverse tunnel (production style)

Used when client is unstable:

```bash id="b9"
autossh -M 0 -f -N -R 9090:localhost:8090 user@192.168.83.50
```

✔ Auto reconnect if connection drops

---

# 🔐 REQUIREMENTS (IMPORTANT)

On SERVER (192.168.83.50):

### 1. Enable remote forwarding

```text id="b10"
sshd_config:
AllowTcpForwarding yes
GatewayPorts yes
```

Restart SSH:

```bash id="b11"
sudo systemctl restart sshd
```

---

# 🔄 FINAL ARCHITECTURE

```text id="b12"
Client (192.168.83.40)
   │
   │  SSH -R tunnel
   ↓
Server (192.168.83.50:9090)
   ↓
Other users access client service via server
```

---

# 🧠 SIMPLE EXPLANATION

👉 You are “publishing” your client service on the server

* Local service = 8090 (client)
* Remote port = 9090 (server)

---

# 🔐 SECURITY SUMMARY

✔ Only SSH (port 22) needed
✔ No direct client exposure
✔ Traffic encrypted
✔ Controlled access via server

---

# ❌ COMMON MISTAKES

* Forgetting `GatewayPorts yes`
* Trying to access server port without binding
* Confusing `-L` and `-R`
* Assuming server runs the service (it doesn’t)

---

# 🏁 FINAL ONE-LINE RULE

```text id="b13"
SSH -R = expose client service → through server → to network
```

